{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b2ae15ba94266c7","type":"tool_result","content":"ai4rag.md:16:**Detailed**: ai4rag provides an end-to-end RAG optimization toolkit that orchestrates document ingestion from S3-compatible storage, text chunking (via Docling and LangChain strategies), embedding generation, vector store indexing (Milvus, PGVector, or Chroma), retrieval, LLM-based answer generation, and evaluation. It uses a hyperparameter optimization loop based on Generalized Additive Models (GAM) to search over chunking parameters, embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval strategies, producing ranked RAG pattern configurations. The library connects to an OpenAI-compatible Model-as-a-Service (MaaS) endpoint for both LLM inference and embedding generation, and includes a KFP event handler for integration with Data Science Pipelines. It is packaged as a Python library with no HTTP server or Kubernetes deployment manifests of its own. [source: pyproject.toml:6-7, 30-44, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:30-62, ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-55, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\nai4rag.md:20:ai4rag is structured as a layered Python library organized around a RAG pipeline lifecycle: data ingestion, chunking, embedding, vector store management, retrieval, LLM generation, and evaluation. The optimization layer wraps this pipeline in a hyperparameter search driven by a GAM-based optimizer that explores combinations of chunking strategies (Docling hierarchical, LangChain recursive), embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval parameters to produce ranked RAG pattern configurations. [source: ai4rag/components/optimization/rag_templates_optimization.py:1-46, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\nai4rag.md:24:The vector store layer supports three pluggable backends — Milvus (with gRPC TLS and PEM certificate materialization), PGVector (PostgreSQL with connection pooling), and Chroma (ephemeral, persistent, or client/server modes) — all configured via environment variables. Credentials for S3 storage, vector stores, and the MaaS endpoint are injected through environment variables, with no Kubernetes Secret manifests defined by the component itself. [source: ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:1-258, ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10-31]\nai4rag.md:94:| pgvector | ~=0.5.0 | Yes | Unknown | Python package dependency |\nai4rag.md:150:| PGVECTOR_PASSWORD | environment variable | Python application | runtime environment | Unknown |\nai4rag.md:181:| **Certificate handling** | System trust store by default; PEM certificate injection for Milvus self-signed endpoints; verify=False fallback for MaaS | ai4rag/rag/vector_store/milvus.py:47-74, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:57-59 |\nai4rag.md:188:- **Embedding and indexing:** Chunked text is sent to an OpenAI-compatible MaaS endpoint for embedding generation, then indexed in the configured vector store (Milvus via gRPC with optional TLS, PGVector via PostgreSQL wire protocol, or Chroma via HTTP). [source: ai4rag/rag/embedding/openai_model.py:31-36, ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:108-177, 181-249]\nai4rag.md:189:- **RAG query and evaluation:** Retrieval queries embed the question via the same MaaS endpoint, retrieve relevant chunks from the vector store, construct a prompt with context, and send it to the foundation model endpoint for LLM chat completion. Evaluation metrics (faithfulness, answer correctness, context correctness) are computed to score each RAG configuration. [source: ai4rag/rag/foundation_models/openai_model.py:25-96, ai4rag/components/optimization/rag_templates_optimization.py:39-46]\n---\n# Component: ai4rag\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Repository**: https://github.com/IBM/ai4rag.git\n- **Version**: ca822e846fcfbdadb130bd74af6336bf448ed432\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Languages**: Python\n- **Deployment Type**: Application Service\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: ai4rag is a Python library that automates the generation and hyperparameter optimization of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) patterns, designed to run as pipeline components within RHOAI Data Science Pipelines. [source: pyproject.toml:6-7, ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-55]\n\n**Detailed**: ai4rag provides an end-to-end RAG optimization toolkit that orchestrates document ingestion from S3-compatible storage, text chunking (via Docling and LangChain strategies), embedding generation, vector store indexing (Milvus, PGVector, or Chroma), retrieval, LLM-based answer generation, and evaluation. It uses a hyperparameter optimization loop based on Generalized Additive Models (GAM) to search over chunking parameters, embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval strategies, producing ranked RAG pattern configurations. The library connects to an OpenAI-compatible Model-as-a-Service (MaaS) endpoint for both LLM inference and embedding generation, and includes a KFP event handler for integration with Data Science Pipelines. It is packaged as a Python library with no HTTP server or Kubernetes deployment manifests of its own. [source: pyproject.toml:6-7, 30-44, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:30-62, ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-55, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nai4rag is structured as a layered Python library organized around a RAG pipeline lifecycle: data ingestion, chunking, embedding, vector store management, retrieval, LLM generation, and evaluation. The optimization layer wraps this pipeline in a hyperparameter search driven by a GAM-based optimizer that explores combinations of chunking strategies (Docling hierarchical, LangChain recursive), embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval parameters to produce ranked RAG pattern configurations. [source: ai4rag/components/optimization/rag_templates_optimization.py:1-46, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\n\nAll LLM and embedding interactions were recently consolidated onto a single OpenAI-compatible client (`create_maas_client`), replacing the previous OGX integration. This client connects to any OpenAI-compatible MaaS endpoint — in the RHOAI context, typically vLLM-based model serving — and includes a TLS fallback that disables certificate verification when self-signed certificates are encountered. This fallback (`verify=False`) allows operation against non-production endpoints but bypasses certificate validation, which is architecturally significant for FIPS and production security posture. [source: ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:30-62]\n\nThe vector store layer supports three pluggable backends — Milvus (with gRPC TLS and PEM certificate materialization), PGVector (PostgreSQL with connection pooling), and Chroma (ephemeral, persistent, or client/server modes) — all configured via environment variables. Credentials for S3 storage, vector stores, and the MaaS endpoint are injected through environment variables, with no Kubernetes Secret manifests defined by the component itself. [source: ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:1-258, ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10-31]\n\nThe library does not expose HTTP endpoints, define Kubernetes services, or ship deployment manifests. It is consumed as an embedded dependency within Kubeflow Pipeline components, with a dedicated `KFPEventHandler` that aggregates status changes and pattern results for post-experiment reporting. [source: ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-70]\n\n## Provenance\n\n### Repo Lineage","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e4171350d16bb3c","agent_id":"a769685b3c6544b0d","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"211f1981-3cb1-4767-b477-bfdf650817dc","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.791Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b2ae15ba94266c7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT