ations, so there is no SQLite→PostgreSQL\n     migration concern and no storage-consolidation epic.\n  4. **Performance scope is dev-experimentation** — no latency SLAs; the benchmark verifies\n     pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index)\n     is acceptable for typical small playground document sets. Open questions about latency\n     thresholds, corpus size ranges, and index type selection are removed.\n  5. **Operator dependency is hard** — self-provisioning via the model-registry-operator pattern\n     requires the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 (In Progress). A go/no-go\n     gate must decide the provisioning approach; this is modeled as an Investigation with\n     conditional branches.\n  6. **Existing remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the majority of effort is\n     infrastructure/provisioning, not vector_io provider integration. OGX requires no code change.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Provisioning approach go/no-go gate for the default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Configure the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 3 (E001–E003). Conditional branch epics: 2. `epic_count` counts all five.\n\nComponent mapping notes (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n- All epics carry `component: Gen AI Studio`. The work lives in the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF\n  (playground configuration) and, for branch A, in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered\n  by RHAISTRAT-7164 — the single most-relevant canonical component for all of it is\n  `Gen AI Studio`; the span is noted here rather than paraphrased into an invalid component name.\n- **OGX Core is passive** (no code changes): the ogx-distribution architecture-context file\n  corroborates that a remote PGVector vector_io backend already exists as an optional integration\n  and that the distribution already strips inline providers (milvus-inline, faiss) in its\n  production build. OGX behavior is validated as acceptance criteria on E002/E003, not as a\n  separate epic — the same team validates it, so the passive-component exception does not fire.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 go/no-go gate] --> BRA[BRANCH-A-E003 operator provisioning]\n    E001 --> BRB[BRANCH-B-E003 BFF-managed provisioning]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 pgvector vector_io config] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 benchmark and sizing]\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The gate outcome (operator layer available) determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all. True gate: `remove` if outcome A does not hold. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The gate outcome selects the fallback provisioning path. True gate: `rewrite` — if BFF-managed provisioning proves unworkable, the further fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (producer → consumer) | The benchmark measures the configured pgvector-backed playground RAG path; it needs the vector_io provider configuration to exist. |\n| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | The vector_io provider configuration consumes a PostgreSQL connection reference regardless of which side provisions the database — the coupling is an acceptance-criterion gate (connection secret available), not a DAG edge. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ branch epics | Branch containment rule | Main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics; both branches produce the same consumable (a per-namespace pgvector connection), expressed as an AC on E002. |\n| No edge E001 → E003 | Step 5.5 / tiered delivery | The benchmark is Tier 1 risk-reduction work: it may run against a manually provisioned pgvector instance in sprint 1 before the selected provisioning path lands. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nRequirements are the strategy's issue-level acceptance criteria (the HLR table is in the missing\nattachment). All are P0; no bundling or priority-split decisions arise.\n\n| HLR | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| AC-1 (P0): default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 (provisioning), E002 (provider configuration) | Exactly one branch executes, selected by E001. |\n| AC-2 (P0): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of capability | E002; verified by E003 | Same surface, same component/team — implementation and its verification are one capability; the benchmark epic verifies rather than rebuilds it. |\n| AC-3 (P0): Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — (removed) | Staff Engineer Input override: Auto Rag decoupled to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Logged above. |\n| AC-4 (P0): vector store runs as a separate process, not inline | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003, E002 | Remote `pgvector` provider is decoupled from the OGX process by design. |\n| AC-5 (P0): scoped for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 | Per-namespace scoping per Staff Engineer Input; the per-instance concern belonged to Auto Rag (out of scope). |\n| AC-6 (P0): lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | E003, BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 | Sizing starts from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted by the sprint-1 benchmark. |\n\nOpen questions disposition:\n- **Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)** — resolved for this strategy by Staff Engineer\n  Input (per-namespace; the per-instance need was Auto Rag's, now out of scope). Captured as an AC\n  on both branch epics. Not an Investigation.\n- **Migration path to a production-grade vector DB** — the answer does not change which epics exist:\n  the implementation consumes a connection reference by design, which is the migration path.\n  Captured as a portability AC on E002 and both branch epics. Not an Investigation.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy attachment** — the full strategy (Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR,\n   Dependencies, NFR tables) was not available; decomposition is based on the issue description,\n   frontmatter links, and Staff Engineer Input. Team assignments are inferred from the\n   `dashboard-crimson-scrum` label. A human should verify epic-to-team assignment against the\n   attached strategy.\n2. **Release-commitment risk (external dependency, uncertain timing)** — RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard\n   operator modularization) is a hard dependency for the primary provisioning path; the Staff\n   Engineer notes it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a release until it delivers.\n   Modeled as conditional branches; the go/no-go decision date is part of E001's deliverable.\n3. **Priority inversion check** — none: strategy priority Blocker, all requirements P0, and every\n   epic is P0.\n4. **Scope trap check** — none found. The Staff Engineer corrections explicitly removed the\n   storage-consolidation and image-build scope traps from the AI-generated strategy.\n\n## Tiered Delivery / Conditional Decomposition\n\nTier 1 (independent of the gate, proceeds immediately):\n- E002 — pgvector vector_io provider configuration (consumes a connection reference; valid for\n  every provisioning outcome, including the future RHOAI 3.6 shared platform database).\n- E003 — sprint-1 benchmark and sizing (may run against a manually provisioned pgvector instance;\n  captures the inline Milvus baseline before inline providers are removed by RHAISTRAT-7474).\n\nTier 2 (gated by E001):\n- Branch A (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time): BRANCH-A-E003 — operator-based self-provisioning\n  following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n- Branch B (it does not): BRANCH-B-E003 — gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning (guardrails-style),\n  with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the last-resort fallback if BFF-managed lifecycle management\n  proves unworkable.\n\nBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document; they are not standalone Jira issues and are\nexcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":82019,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e4171350d16bb3c","agent_id":"a769685b3c6544b0d","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"d7dba5f8-069e-42d1-bc4a-74dbfd2a8fc8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:26:27.342Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
