{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-866cd62c-4fd2-423e-ba56-081292c5038c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-af80653d56198bb8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-7pc41hkd/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Provisioning approach go/no-go gate for the default vector store\n\n## Description\n\nResolves the strategy's central uncertainty before the provisioning work is committed: whether the\nRHOAI-controlled operator layer (the dashboard operator modularization tracked as RHAISTRAT-7164,\nIn Progress) will be available in time for the target release, and therefore which provisioning\napproach the Gen AI Studio default vector store uses — operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector\nself-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern (primary path), or gen-ai\nBFF-managed provisioning (fallback path), with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the last-resort\nfallback. The Staff Engineer Input directs the development team to define this go/no-go gate as\npart of sprint planning; this epic makes that decision a first-class deliverable.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the go/no-go criteria and the decision date for the RHAISTRAT-7164 dependency (what\n  \"lands in time\" means for the target release commitment).\n- Track RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery status against those criteria.\n- Evaluate the fallback path's known caveats so the decision is informed rather than defaulted:\n  cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation in a\n  BFF that is not an operator with a reconciliation loop.\n- Confirm the portability requirement that applies to whichever path is chosen: the consuming\n  configuration must be able to switch to the shared platform-provided PostgreSQL connection\n  string when it becomes available (RHOAI 3.6) without rework.\n- Record the decision (chosen approach, rationale, criteria, date) and communicate it to the\n  Gen AI Studio lead and engineering team.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision exists that states the go/no-go criteria, the decision date, the tracked\n  status of RHAISTRAT-7164 against those criteria, and the selected provisioning approach.\n- The decision is unambiguous: a downstream implementer can tell, from the decision alone, whether\n  operator-based self-provisioning or BFF-managed provisioning is being built (or that the\n  bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback is being taken, and why).\n- The fallback-path caveats (lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop) are evaluated\n  and the evaluation is referenced by the decision.\n- The decision is reviewed with the Gen AI Studio lead and the engineering team (sprint planning).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): determines which provisioning\n  epic exists and what it does.\n- Informs AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping): confirms per-namespace scoping for Gen AI Studio.\n- Governs the strategy's success criterion that RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) is\n  unblocked by a viable platform-level replacement.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The gating input (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing) is externally controlled; this epic's\n  deliverable is the decision and its criteria, not the dependency's delivery. Schedule risk from\n  dependency slippage belongs here as a note, not as an acceptance criterion.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":84975,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e4171350d16bb3c","agent_id":"a769685b3c6544b0d","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"0c6465da-3950-4355-acb3-814d06a3f7dc","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:26:41.343Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
