{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7ed67bed-ec0a-45ca-af14-b6a828a962e3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-99cd8b0a4078f477","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-7pc41hkd/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B)\n\n## Description\n\nBranch B of the provisioning go/no-go gate — executed if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer\n(RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for the target release. The gen-ai BFF in odh-dashboard\nprovisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, following the pattern the BFF already uses\nto provision guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\nPostgreSQL exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret for a\npgvector-capable PostgreSQL instance. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation\nloop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential\nrotation) receives explicit design attention in this epic.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Gen-ai BFF provisioning logic that creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret\n  (existing Red Hat postgresql-16 image with pgvector — no custom image build, no pipeline\n  onboarding) when a RAG-enabled playground is configured and no instance exists in the namespace.\n- Idempotency: reuse an existing suitable instance rather than provisioning a duplicate.\n- Lifecycle design for a non-operator provisioning actor: cleanup on playground deletion,\n  behavior on pod restarts, and credential rotation handling, each explicitly designed and\n  documented.\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL values,\n  adjustable from the sprint-1 benchmark results.\n- Image reference resolved through the existing related-image environment variable for\n  disconnected installations.\n- Portability by design: the BFF consumes a connection reference so that, when the\n  platform-provided shared database becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), it switches to the shared\n  connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace that has no suitable PostgreSQL results in a\n  per-namespace pgvector-capable instance being provisioned, with no manual user setup.\n- Provisioning is idempotent: reconfiguring or adding another RAG-enabled playground in the same\n  namespace reuses the existing instance.\n- The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not an\n  inline provider).\n- Deleting the owning playground cleans up the provisioned resources, and the designed behavior\n  for pod restarts and credential rotation is implemented and documented.\n- No custom container image is built: the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image is used, referenced\n  via the platform related-image variable so disconnected installs work.\n- Resource requests/limits follow the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL values as the starting\n  point, with any adjustment from the sprint-1 benchmark documented.\n- The configuration source is a swappable connection reference: switching to the shared platform\n  database connection string requires no rework of the provisioning-adjacent configuration.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): provisioning half of the requirement.\n- AC-4 (runs as a separate process, not inline).\n- AC-5 (scoped for multi-tenant environments, per-namespace isolation).\n- AC-6 (lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments): resource sizing scope.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This branch exists only if the go/no-go gate selects the fallback path. If BFF-managed\n  lifecycle management proves unworkable during design, the further fallback is requiring users\n  to bring their own PostgreSQL (manual OGXServer connection configuration), consistent with the\n  breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":87794,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e4171350d16bb3c","agent_id":"a769685b3c6544b0d","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"3af0cfa0-c952-4deb-a6ec-d39a339fdf54","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:27:28.175Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
