{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97b0e4d6439de085","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004: Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n11\t\n12\tStrategy priority: Blocker → all mapped requirements are P0. No priority split applies.\n13\t\n14\t## Input Fidelity Notes\n15\t\n16\t- The full strategy body is stored as a Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not\n17\t  available in this environment (Jira fetch is disabled; only the issue description was provisioned).\n18\t  The Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables could not be parsed.\n19\t  Requirements were derived from the issue-level Acceptance Criteria, and team assignments were\n20\t  inferred from the strategy's `dashboard-crimson-scrum` label. See Health Warnings.\n21\t- The **Staff Engineer Input** section is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated\n22\t  strategy. Overrides applied (logged per Step 1.5):\n23\t  1. **Auto Rag decoupled** — removed from scope; Auto Rag/pgvector compatibility is owned by\n24\t     RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Acceptance criterion AC-3 is therefore out of scope here.\n25\t  2. **No custom image build, no Konflux onboarding** — `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`\n26\t     already includes pgvector, and `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already in the\n27\t     rhods-operator CSV. The strategy's 1–2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated, so no\n28\t     onboarding-chain epics (repo/license/konflux) are generated.\n29\t  3. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — app storage stays on SQLite; introducing\n30\t     PostgreSQL for vectors is new work. Vector storage (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`)\n31\t     and application storage are independent configurations, so there is no SQLite→PostgreSQL\n32\t     migration concern and no storage-consolidation epic.\n33\t  4. **Performance scope is dev-experimentation** — no latency SLAs; the benchmark verifies\n34\t     pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index)\n35\t     is acceptable for typical small playground document sets. Open questions about latency\n36\t     thresholds, corpus size ranges, and index type selection are removed.\n37\t  5. **Operator dependency is hard** — self-provisioning via the model-registry-operator pattern\n38\t     requires the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 (In Progress). A go/no-go\n39\t     gate must decide the provisioning approach; this is modeled as an Investigation with\n40\t     conditional branches.\n41\t  6. **Existing remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the majority of effort is\n42\t     infrastructure/provisioning, not vector_io provider integration. OGX requires no code change.\n43\t\n44\t## Epic List\n45\t\n46\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n47\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Provisioning approach go/no-go gate for the default vector store | Investigation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Configure the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B) | Implementation | dashboard-crimson-scrum | P0 |\n53\t\n54\tMain-plan epics: 3 (E001–E003). Conditional branch epics: 2. `epic_count` counts all five.\n55\t\n56\tComponent mapping notes (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n57\t- All epics carry `component: Gen AI Studio`. The work lives in the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF\n58\t  (playground configuration) and, for branch A, in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered\n59\t  by RHAISTRAT-7164 — the single most-relevant canonical component for all of it is\n60\t  `Gen AI Studio`; the span is noted here rather than paraphrased into an invalid component name.\n61\t- **OGX Core is passive** (no code changes): the ogx-distribution architecture-context file\n62\t  corroborates that a remote PGVector vector_io backend already exists as an optional integration\n63\t  and that the distribution already strips inline providers (milvus-inline, faiss) in its\n64\t  production build. OGX behavior is validated as acceptance criteria on E002/E003, not as a\n65\t  separate epic — the same team validates it, so the passive-component exception does not fire.\n66\t\n67\t## Dependency DAG\n68\t\n69\t```mermaid\n70\tgraph TD\n71\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 go/no-go gate] --> BRA[BRANCH-A-E003 operator provisioning]\n72\t    E001 --> BRB[BRANCH-B-E003 BFF-managed provisioning]\n73\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 pgvector vector_io config] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 benchmark and sizing]\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\tArrows point from dependency to dependent.\n77\t\n78\t## DAG Justification\n79\t\n80\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n81\t|------|------|-----------|\n82\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The gate outcome (operator layer available) determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all. True gate: `remove` if outcome A does not hold. |\n83\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The gate outcome selects the fallback provisioning path. True gate: `rewrite` — if BFF-managed provisioning proves unworkable, the further fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL. |\n84\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (producer → consumer) | The benchmark measures the configured pgvector-backed playground RAG path; it needs the vector_io provider configuration to exist. |\n85\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | The vector_io provider configuration consumes a PostgreSQL connection reference regardless of which side provisions the database — the coupling is an acceptance-criterion gate (connection secret available), not a DAG edge. |\n86\t| No edge E002 ↔ branch epics | Branch containment rule | Main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics; both branches produce the same consumable (a per-namespace pgvector connection), expressed as an AC on E002. |\n87\t| No edge E001 → E003 | Step 5.5 / tiered delivery | The benchmark is Tier 1 risk-reduction work: it may run against a manually provisioned pgvector instance in sprint 1 before the selected provisioning path lands. |\n88\t\n89\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n90\t\n91\tRequirements are the strategy's issue-level acceptance criteria (the HLR table is in the missing\n92\tattachment). All are P0; no bundling or priority-split decisions arise.\n93\t\n94\t| HLR | Epic(s) | Notes |\n95\t|-----|---------|-------|\n96\t| AC-1 (P0): default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 (provisioning), E002 (provider configuration) | Exactly one branch executes, selected by E001. |\n97\t| AC-2 (P0): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of capability | E002; verified by E003 | Same surface, same component/team — implementation and its verification are one capability; the benchmark epic verifies rather than rebuilds it. |\n98\t| AC-3 (P0): Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — (removed) | Staff Engineer Input override: Auto Rag decoupled to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Logged above. |\n99\t| AC-4 (P0): vector store runs as a separate process, not inline | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003, E002 | Remote `pgvector` provider is decoupled from the OGX process by design. |\n100\t| AC-5 (P0): scoped for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 | Per-namespace scoping per Staff Engineer Input; the per-instance concern belonged to Auto Rag (out of scope). |\n101\t| AC-6 (P0): lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | E003, BRANCH-A-E003 / BRANCH-B-E003 | Sizing starts from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted by the sprint-1 benchmark. |\n102\t\n103\tOpen questions disposition:\n104\t- **Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)** — resolved for this strategy by Staff Engineer\n105\t  Input (per-namespace; the per-instance need was Auto Rag's, now out of scope). Captured as an AC\n106\t  on both branch epics. Not an Investigation.\n107\t- **Migration path to a production-grade vector DB** — the answer does not change which epics exist:\n108\t  the implementation consumes a connection reference by design, which is the migration path.\n109\t  Captured as a portability AC on E002 and both branch epics. Not an Investigation.\n110\t\n111\t## Health Warnings\n112\t\n113\t1. **Missing strategy attachment** — the full strategy (Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR,\n114\t   Dependencies, NFR tables) was not available; decomposition is based on the issue description,\n115\t   frontmatter links, and Staff Engineer Input. Team assignments are inferred from the\n116\t   `dashboard-crimson-scrum` label. A human should verify epic-to-team assignment against the\n117\t   attached strategy.\n118\t2. **Release-commitment risk (external dependency, uncertain timing)** — RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard\n119\t   operator modularization) is a hard dependency for the primary provisioning path; the Staff\n120\t   Engineer notes it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a release until it delivers.\n121\t   Modeled as conditional branches; the go/no-go decision date is part of E001's deliverable.\n122\t3. **Priority inversion check** — none: strategy priority Blocker, all requirements P0, and every\n123\t   epic is P0.\n124\t4. **Scope trap check** — none found. The Staff Engineer corrections explicitly removed the\n125\t   storage-consolidation and image-build scope traps from the AI-generated strategy.\n126\t\n127\t## Tiered Delivery / Conditional Decomposition\n128\t\n129\tTier 1 (independent of the gate, proceeds immediately):\n130\t- E002 — pgvector vector_io provider configuration (consumes a connection reference; valid for\n131\t  every provisioning outcome, including the future RHOAI 3.6 shared platform database).\n132\t- E003 — sprint-1 benchmark and sizing (may run against a manually provisioned pgvector instance;\n133\t  captures the inline Milvus baseline before inline providers are removed by RHAISTRAT-7474).\n134\t\n135\tTier 2 (gated by E001):\n136\t- Branch A (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time): BRANCH-A-E003 — operator-based self-provisioning\n137\t  following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n138\t- Branch B (it does not): BRANCH-B-E003 — gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning (guardrails-style),\n139\t  with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the last-resort fallback if BFF-managed lifecycle management\n140\t  proves unworkable.\n141\t\n142\tBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document; they are not standalone Jira issues and are\n143\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac7f19a9efafe13c","agent_id":"a7ed42b29d881bc12","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"b41e2b2e-4138-4bc3-a11d-c71abd8d8130","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:29:51.423Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-97b0e4d6439de085","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
