{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-978ba407c15219e2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: 'If BFF-managed lifecycle management proves unworkable during\n16\t    design, rewrite to the bring-your-own PostgreSQL fallback: users manually configure\n17\t    the OGXServer connection details, consistent with the breaking changes introduced\n18\t    in OGX 1.0.'\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch B)\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tBranch B of the provisioning go/no-go gate — executed if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer\n40\t(RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for the target release. The gen-ai BFF in odh-dashboard\n41\tprovisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, following the pattern the BFF already uses\n42\tto provision guardrails infrastructure: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\n43\tPostgreSQL exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret for a\n44\tpgvector-capable PostgreSQL instance. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation\n45\tloop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential\n46\trotation) receives explicit design attention in this epic.\n47\t\n48\t## Scope\n49\t\n50\t- Gen-ai BFF provisioning logic that creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret\n51\t  (existing Red Hat postgresql-16 image with pgvector — no custom image build, no pipeline\n52\t  onboarding) when a RAG-enabled playground is configured and no instance exists in the namespace.\n53\t- Idempotency: reuse an existing suitable instance rather than provisioning a duplicate.\n54\t- Lifecycle design for a non-operator provisioning actor: cleanup on playground deletion,\n55\t  behavior on pod restarts, and credential rotation handling, each explicitly designed and\n56\t  documented.\n57\t- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL values,\n58\t  adjustable from the sprint-1 benchmark results.\n59\t- Image reference resolved through the existing related-image environment variable for\n60\t  disconnected installations.\n61\t- Portability by design: the BFF consumes a connection reference so that, when the\n62\t  platform-provided shared database becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), it switches to the shared\n63\t  connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n64\t\n65\t## Acceptance Criteria\n66\t\n67\t- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace that has no suitable PostgreSQL results in a\n68\t  per-namespace pgvector-capable instance being provisioned, with no manual user setup.\n69\t- Provisioning is idempotent: reconfiguring or adding another RAG-enabled playground in the same\n70\t  namespace reuses the existing instance.\n71\t- The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not an\n72\t  inline provider).\n73\t- Deleting the owning playground cleans up the provisioned resources, and the designed behavior\n74\t  for pod restarts and credential rotation is implemented and documented.\n75\t- No custom container image is built: the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image is used, referenced\n76\t  via the platform related-image variable so disconnected installs work.\n77\t- Resource requests/limits follow the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL values as the starting\n78\t  point, with any adjustment from the sprint-1 benchmark documented.\n79\t- The configuration source is a swappable connection reference: switching to the shared platform\n80\t  database connection string requires no rework of the provisioning-adjacent configuration.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability\n83\t\n84\t- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): provisioning half of the requirement.\n85\t- AC-4 (runs as a separate process, not inline).\n86\t- AC-5 (scoped for multi-tenant environments, per-namespace isolation).\n87\t- AC-6 (lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments): resource sizing scope.\n88\t\n89\t## Risks / Notes\n90\t\n91\t- This branch exists only if the go/no-go gate selects the fallback path. If BFF-managed\n92\t  lifecycle management proves unworkable during design, the further fallback is requiring users\n93\t  to bring their own PostgreSQL (manual OGXServer connection configuration), consistent with the\n94\t  breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac7f19a9efafe13c","agent_id":"a7ed42b29d881bc12","session_id":"eb96f7e6-35e1-46c5-82db-a48f5def6ca9","uuid":"30f86112-75c7-42be-92c0-c2db00e7f063","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:30:00.140Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-978ba407c15219e2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
